# Work folders: where each file goes

Open this when you are about to create a file and it is not obviously a question,
conjecture, source or experiment. The index is [work/_index.md](../../work/_index.md).

## Why

A project that runs for weeks produces hundreds of working files: lemmas in progress,
estimates, plans, briefs, check scripts. Dropped into one flat folder they cannot be
navigated: the next session cannot tell a live document from a superseded one, which line
of work a file belongs to, or what a finished line of work established. Each kind of file
therefore has one home.

## Where each file goes

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| a line of work that is not an experiment (a proof campaign, a family of estimates, a literature pass) | its own folder, `work/<slug>/`, copied from `work/TEMPLATE/` |
| its plan, RESUME HERE and progress log | `work/<slug>/README.md` |
{{#if modules.attempts}}
| each line of attack on it: where it broke, what it established, what would revive it | `wiki/attempts/`, through `bin/attempts` |
{{/if}}
| its working documents, proofs in progress, staging record | `work/<slug>/` |
| a small script that checks a computation for it | `work/<slug>/checks/` |
| a brief for an agent, a throwaway note or check (never a manuscript) | `work/<slug>/scratch/` (git ignores it) |
{{#if modules.experiments}}
| a computation with a driver, runs and an analysis | an experiment, `experiments/<slug>/` |
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.commit_gate}}
| a reviewer's verdict text | nowhere: pipe it into `bin/gate-stamp ... -`, which keeps it in `.gates/stamps.jsonl` |
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.literature}}
| what a source says and what we take from it | the literature wiki (the `ingest` skill) |
{{/if}}
| a standing note about how the project works | `notes/` (the workflow journal{{#if modules.program_review}}, program goals and reviews{{/if}}) |

**One folder per line of work, named for what it is trying to establish**
(`work/corner-smoothing-mechanism/`), not for a date or a session. {{#if modules.attempts}}A new line of attack on the
same goal gets an attempt record (`bin/attempts new work/<slug>#<brick> "<approach>"`) whose
target names this folder, not a new folder; the README links them.{{else}}A new attempt at the same
goal is a new section in the same README, not a new folder.{{/if}}
*Why:* a folder per goal is what lets the next reader find everything about it in one place.
*Anti-scope:* a one-step task that produces a single file does not need a folder; put the
file in the folder of the work it serves.

**Keep the README current.** Its RESUME HERE names the exact next step; its `## Files`
section says what each file holds and whether it is a draft, reviewed or superseded.
*Why:* the README is what a fresh session reads instead of opening every file.

**Close a folder when its work ends.** Set `status: done` or `parked` in the README and in
`work/_index.md`, and write the **Outcome**: what was established and at what strength,
what failed and why, and where each result now lives{{#if modules.conjectures}} (a conjecture file{{#if modules.known_results}}, a
known result{{/if}}, a write-up){{/if}}. A result that exists only inside a work folder is not yet
part of the project's record.
{{#if modules.questions}}
When a question is parked or answered, close the work folders that serve it in the same
change.
{{/if}}
*Why:* an open folder whose work stopped weeks ago reads as live, and the next agent resumes
it.
*Anti-scope:* do not delete a closed folder's files; they are the record of how the result
was reached.

**Scratch is scratch.** Nothing in a `scratch/` folder may be cited or relied on: it is not
committed. Anything worth keeping moves out of it into the work folder; a manuscript never
starts there. A reminder fires when a `.tex` or `.bib` file is created in a `scratch/`
folder, and `bin/doctor`{{#if modules.commit_gate}} and the commit gate warn{{else}} warns{{/if}} about a manuscript or a week-old file left
in one.
*Why:* git ignores `scratch/`, so a draft kept there is in no commit: it is lost with the
folder and invisible to every review.
